# -*- coding: utf-8 -*-
"""
PI E-710 3CD — Qudi ScanningProbeInterface Hardware Module
==========================================================

Controls a Physik Instrumente E-710 3CD piezo controller via the PI GCS DLL
(NI-GPIB) and exposes a complete ScanningProbeInterface for use with Qudi's
confocal scanning probe logic and GUI.

═══════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════
 REQUIRED EXTERNAL HARDWARE — PHOTON COUNTING
═══════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════
 The PI E-710 outputs one TTL trigger pulse at each scan pixel.
 You MUST connect an external photon counter to collect photons.

 Wiring
 ──────
   PI E-710  Trigger OUT  ────►  Counter  Gate / Clock IN
   SPCM / APD  TTL OUT   ────►  Counter  Source / Edge IN

 Trigger polarity (config option 'trigger_mode'):
   'SPCM'  →  active-low  short pulse  (PI firmware sends  1KT-1)
   'APD'   →  active-high long  pulse  (PI firmware sends   1KT2)

 Supported counter examples:
   • NI-DAQ counter (nidaqmx, e.g. PCIe-6323, USB-6363)
   • Swabian Instruments Time Tagger (CountBetweenMarkers)
   • PicoQuant HydraHarp 400 / MultiHarp 150
   • Custom FPGA-based photon counter

 Implementation guide
 ────────────────────
 Fill in the three placeholder methods at the bottom of this file:
   _arm_photon_counter(n_pixels, t_pixel)   ← called BEFORE PI scan starts
   _read_photon_counts(n_pixels)            ← called AFTER PI scan ends
   _stop_photon_counter()                   ← called on abort / emergency stop

═══════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════
 BACK SCAN
═══════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════
 Not supported.  The PI performs forward + backward sweeps automatically,
 but photon gating is only active on the forward sweep (by design of the
 segment/trigger protocol).  configure_back_scan() will raise ValueError.

═══════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════
 SUPPORTED SCAN TYPES
═══════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════
 1D :  axes = ('x',)      ('y',)      ('z',)
 2D :  axes = ('x','y')   ('x','z')   ('y','z')
       First element is always the FAST axis.

 Note: scan_xz() and scan_yz() in PIE710Controller have the trigger mode
 hard-coded to active-low (SPCM style, i.e. '1KT-1').  Only XY, X, Y, Z
 scans honour the 'trigger_mode' configuration option.

═══════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════
 QUDI CONFIGURATION  (qudi config yaml)
═══════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════
 hardware:
     pi_e710_scanner:
         module.Class: 'pi_e710_scanning_probe.PIE710ScanningProbe'
         options:
             dll_path:     'C:/PI/E7XX_GCS_DLL_x64.dll'
             gpib_board:   0
             gpib_address: 4
             x_range: [0.0, 100.0]   # µm  (overridden by hardware limits)
             y_range: [0.0, 100.0]   # µm
             z_range: [0.0,  50.0]   # µm
             trigger_mode: 'SPCM'    # 'SPCM' (active-low) or 'APD' (active-high)
═══════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════

Dependencies
────────────
  pip install numpy
  PI GCS DLL  (E7XX_GCS_DLL_x64.dll, supplied by Physik Instrumente)
  NI-GPIB driver + NI-488.2 runtime
  pie710_controller.py  ← place in the same hardware directory as this file

